</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS3.6" class="ltx_text">III-C</span> </span><span id="S3.SS3.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Teacher-Student Distillation</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To bridge the sim-to-real gap, we implement a teacher-student training pipeline similar to <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite>. We train the teacher policy using the dynamic reward curriculum, leveraging accurate measurements obtained from the simulation. Then, we train the student policy using fixed rewards and a policy distillation approach, which minimizes the  <span title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">KL</span> (<abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">KL</abbr>) divergence between its action distribution and that of the teacher policy <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite>. Unlike the teacher, the student policy operates with noisy measurements, lacks access to stage-related information, and must deduce the <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">EE</abbr> pose from a history of the arm’s <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">DOF</abbr>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The teacher policy may occasionally fail to recover from situations not encountered during training. For instance, due to the constraints imposed by the stage graph in <a href="#S3.F3" title="In III-B1 Stage Transition Graph ‣ III-B Dynamic Reward Curriculum ‣ III Method ‣ Collaborative Loco-Manipulation for Pick-and-Place Tasks with Dynamic Reward Curriculum" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, the robots cannot transition back to <span id="S3.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">S0</span> after an external disturbance at <span id="S3.SS3.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">S4</span> causes the object to fall far from the target. In contrast, the student policy determines its stage solely based on external observations, enabling robots to recognize the new configuration as <span id="S3.SS3.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">S0</span> and autonomously restart the <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">PnP</abbr> task.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">IV </span><span id="S4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Results</span></h2>

<figure id="S4.F4" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.13239v1/hardware_full.png" id="S4.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:429/184;" width="429" height="184" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 4: </span>
ANYmal D robots performing <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">PnP</abbr> tasks in single-robot (<span id="S4.F4.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">top</span>) and dual-robot (<span id="S4.F4.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">middle</span>) setups. The robot dynamically adapts to perturbations by re-grasping a displaced box (<span id="S4.F4.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">bottom</span>).
</figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S4.SS1.6" class="ltx_text">IV-A</span> </span><span id="S4.SS1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Implementation Details.</span></h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We define the <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">PnP</abbr> operation as successful if the box is delivered within <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.5\text{\,}\mathrm{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.5</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi class="ltx_unit">m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.5\text{\,}\mathrm{m}</annotation></semantics></math> of the goal location. We trigger failure if any robot falls or if the task is not completed within <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="45\text{\,}\mathrm{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>45</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi class="ltx_unit">s</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">45\text{\,}\mathrm{s}</annotation></semantics></math>. We randomly initialize the robots and objects around the goal position at distances ranging from <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.6</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.6</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5</annotation></semantics></math> meters.
We also use boxes with different weights and shapes during training. For training single-robot <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">PnP</abbr>, we use three different box assets with masses ranging from <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1.0\text{\,}\mathrm{kg}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.0</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi class="ltx_unit">kg</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.0\text{\,}\mathrm{kg}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1.3\text{\,}\mathrm{kg}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.3</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi class="ltx_unit">kg</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.3\text{\,}\mathrm{kg}</annotation></semantics></math> and edge dimensions from <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.22\text{\,}\mathrm{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.22</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi class="ltx_unit">m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.22\text{\,}\mathrm{m}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.45\text{\,}\mathrm{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.45</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi class="ltx_unit">m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.45\text{\,}\mathrm{m}</annotation></semantics></math>. For dual-robot <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">PnP</abbr>, we use two different box assets with <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1.9\text{\,}\mathrm{kg}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.9</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi class="ltx_unit">kg</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.9\text{\,}\mathrm{kg}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2.2\text{\,}\mathrm{kg}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2.2</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi class="ltx_unit">kg</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2.2\text{\,}\mathrm{kg}</annotation></semantics></math> weights, respectively, and edge dimensions from <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.3\text{\,}\mathrm{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.3</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi class="ltx_unit">m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.3\text{\,}\mathrm{m}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1.5\text{\,}\mathrm{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.5</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi class="ltx_unit">m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.5\text{\,}\mathrm{m}</annotation></semantics></math>. All box dimensions are randomly scaled with a factor between 0.8 and 1.2. For training, we use the  <span title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">MA</span> (<abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">MA</abbr>) adaptation of the PPO algorithm <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>]</cite> as described in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>. We conduct all our simulation experiments in Isaac Gym simulation environments <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite> with different starting configurations and object sizes.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S4.SS2.6" class="ltx_text">IV-B</span> </span><span id="S4.SS2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Hardware Experiments</span></h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We test our policies in single- and dual-robot experiments using ANYmal D platforms equipped with a 6-<abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">DOF</abbr> robotic arm DynaArm and the Robotiq 2F-140 gripper <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>]</cite>. We task the robots with picking up and carrying either a <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1.5\text{\,}\mathrm{kg}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.5</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi class="ltx_unit">kg</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.5\text{\,}\mathrm{kg}</annotation></semantics></math> box (single-robot) or a <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2.0\text{\,}\mathrm{kg}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2.0</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi class="ltx_unit">kg</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2.0\text{\,}\mathrm{kg}</annotation></semantics></math> box (dual-robot) to a given target location. We provide snapshots of our experiments in <a href="#S4.F4" title="In IV Results ‣ Collaborative Loco-Manipulation for Pick-and-Place Tasks with Dynamic Reward Curriculum" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, where the goal position is marked on the ground using yellow tape. We implement multi-robot communications using the gRPC framework <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>, which serializes messages between a server and its clients distributed across different machines; we refer the reader to <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite> for implementation details. Finally, we estimate the pose of the box with respect to the robot bases through a motion capture system.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The single-agent policy succeeded in 8 of 9 trials, and the dual-agent policy in all 3 initial runs. In 3 additional trials, the robots delivered the box but stalled before drop-off due to mocap dropouts. We test the re-grasping behavior in the single-agent setup by moving the box around during the <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">PnP</abbr> execution. As shown in the bottom row of <a href="#S4.F4" title="In IV Results ‣ Collaborative Loco-Manipulation for Pick-and-Place Tasks with Dynamic Reward Curriculum" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, the robot adapts dynamically by pursuing the displaced object, re-establishing its grasp, and completing the operation. These results highlight the system’s reliability and robustness in realistic settings. We provide videos of the experiments in the supplementary materials.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F5" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.13239v1/stage_percentages_shades.svg" id="S4.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/235;" width="476" height="235"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 5: </span>Stage progression in a dual-robot <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">PnP</abbr> training with 2048 environments, showing the percentage ending in each stage across episodes. Four shaded sections highlight training falling back to previous stages for enhanced learning and cross-stage awareness. (<span id="S4.F5.16" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">A</span>) The percentage of <span id="S4.F5.17" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">S0</span> increases when reaching <span id="S4.F5.18" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">S2</span>. (<span id="S4.F5.19" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">B</span>) The percentages of <span id="S4.F5.20" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">S0</span>, <span id="S4.F5.21" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">S1</span>, and <span id="S4.F5.22" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">S2</span> increase when reaching <span id="S4.F5.23" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">S3</span>. (<span id="S4.F5.24" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">C</span>) The percentage of <span id="S4.F5.25" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">S3</span> increases between iteration 2800 and 3800 when reaching <span id="S4.F5.26" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">S4</span>. (<span id="S4.F5.27" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">D</span>) The percentage of <span id="S4.F5.28" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">S2</span> increases when reaching <span id="S4.F5.29" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">S5</span>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S4.F6" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.13239v1/saliency.png" id="S4.F6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:429/173;" width="429" height="173" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 6: </span>
Saliency maps for selected observations at various stages of dual-robot <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">PnP</abbr>. The ego robot is represented in red, with its neighbor in gold. Handle 1 is shown in gold, and handle 2 in grey. Lighter shades in the saliency map indicate more influential observations in guiding the policy’s actions, while darker shades denote less relevant observations. The snapshots from A to E depict key stages of the task: reaching, lifting, transporting to the target location (at the red cross mark), laying, and releasing the object.
</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S4.SS3.6" class="ltx_text">IV-C</span> </span><span id="S4.SS3.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Analysis of Stage Progression During Training</span></h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Training a single policy for long-horizon, multi-stage problems is challenging: the policy’s action distribution must remain flexible enough for learning new tasks at later stages, yet not overfit to them so as to forget earlier ones. Moreover, the policy should be able to refine early-stage motions to facilitate later tasks, as well as acquire recovery strategies when those tasks fail. By analyzing the stage progression during a representative training in <a href="#S4.F5" title="In IV-B Hardware Experiments ‣ IV Results ‣ Collaborative Loco-Manipulation for Pick-and-Place Tasks with Dynamic Reward Curriculum" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, we show the benefits of our dynamic reward curriculum in addressing the above problems in dual-robot <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">PnP</abbr>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS3.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S4.SS3.SSS1.6" class="ltx_text">IV-C</span>1 </span>Refining Motions for Later Stages</h4>

<div id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In area A of <a href="#S4.F5" title="In IV-B Hardware Experiments ‣ IV Results ‣ Collaborative Loco-Manipulation for Pick-and-Place Tasks with Dynamic Reward Curriculum" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, stage progression reveals how motion refinement in earlier stages contributes to the successful completion of later ones. Specifically, with reference to <a href="#S3.F3" title="In III-B1 Stage Transition Graph ‣ III-B Dynamic Reward Curriculum ‣ III Method ‣ Collaborative Loco-Manipulation for Pick-and-Place Tasks with Dynamic Reward Curriculum" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, both robots begin at <span id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">S1</span> (“Gripper ready”) and are learning to grasp the handles (<span id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">S2</span>) after having aligned their grippers above them. However, lowering the grippers without precise alignment may violate the condition of <span id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">S1</span>, forcing a backward transition to <span id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">S0</span> (“Idle”). When this occurs, the robots are required to re-optimize the <span id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">S0</span> rewards, thus re-establishing correct alignment above the handles. This repeated emphasis leads to smoother grasping motions, ensuring the grippers approach the handles from above rather than from the side, thereby avoiding collisions and deadlocks during execution.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S4.SS3.SSS2.6" class="ltx_text">IV-C</span>2 </span>Learning Recovery Behaviors</h4>

<div id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our experiments revealed the emergence of recovery behaviors following disruptive events, as discussed in <a href="#S4.SS2" title="IV-B Hardware Experiments ‣ IV Results ‣ Collaborative Loco-Manipulation for Pick-and-Place Tasks with Dynamic Reward Curriculum" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">IV-B</span></a>. This is especially visible in area B of <a href="#S4.F5" title="In IV-B Hardware Experiments ‣ IV Results ‣ Collaborative Loco-Manipulation for Pick-and-Place Tasks with Dynamic Reward Curriculum" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>: when attempting to lift the object from <span id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">S2</span> (“Object grabbed”), unstable grasp configurations—still considered valid for <span id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">S2</span>—often cause the object to slip, resulting in a fallback to <span id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">S0</span>. However, the increasing percentages in <span id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">S1</span>–<span id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">S2</span> indicate that the robots learn to recover by re-attempting the grasp after such failures. Furthermore, the comparable proportions of episodes across stages <span id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">S0</span>–<span id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">S3</span> (<math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="20\text{\,}\mathrm{\%}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>20</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi class="ltx_unit">%</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">20\text{\,}\mathrm{\%}</annotation></semantics></math>–<math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="40\text{\,}\mathrm{\%}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>40</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi class="ltx_unit">%</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">40\text{\,}\mathrm{\%}</annotation></semantics></math>) suggest that the combined rewards for approaching, grasping, and lifting promote the rejection of unstable grasping strategies, producing smooth, forward-looking motions.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S4.SS3.SSS3.6" class="ltx_text">IV-C</span>3 </span>Overcoming Stage Bottlenecks</h4>

<div id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Having reached <span id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">S4</span> (“Object on goal”), learning <span id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">S5</span> (“Object placed”) is particularly challenging, as it requires two robots to coordinate the lowering motion, directly conflicting with the previously learned lifting behavior. PPO struggles in this setting because the necessary actions lie in a low-probability region of the learned distribution. As shown in area C of <a href="#S4.F5" title="In IV-B Hardware Experiments ‣ IV Results ‣ Collaborative Loco-Manipulation for Pick-and-Place Tasks with Dynamic Reward Curriculum" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, the consequent lack of immediate rewards for the <span id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">S4<math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math>S5</span> transition makes reaching <span id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">S4</span> appear unprofitable. Thus, the optimizer may maximize the overall return by avoiding <span id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">S4</span> altogether and repeatedly exploiting <span id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">S3<math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math>S4</span> rewards instead. Our dynamic reward curriculum mitigates this regression by adaptively scaling up the <span id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">S3</span> rewards, thereby pushing the policy past the bottleneck and enabling steady progress, as evidenced by the shifts around 2800 and 3800 training iterations in <a href="#S4.F5" title="In IV-B Hardware Experiments ‣ IV Results ‣ Collaborative Loco-Manipulation for Pick-and-Place Tasks with Dynamic Reward Curriculum" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3.SSS4" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S4.SS3.SSS4.6" class="ltx_text">IV-C</span>4 </span>Mitigating Forgetting</h4>

<div id="S4.SS3.SSS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Once <span id="S4.SS3.SSS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">S5</span> is learned, policies in area D of <a href="#S4.F5" title="In IV-B Hardware Experiments ‣ IV Results ‣ Collaborative Loco-Manipulation for Pick-and-Place Tasks with Dynamic Reward Curriculum" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> tend to forget previously acquired lifting motions, causing more episodes to terminate at <span id="S4.SS3.SSS4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">S2</span>. By re-emphasizing lifting rewards, our curriculum enables robots to consistently perform both lifting at <span id="S4.SS3.SSS4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">S2</span> and placing at <span id="S4.SS3.SSS4.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">S4</span>—a difficult pair of opposing motions for a single <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">RL</abbr> policy.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S4.SS4.6" class="ltx_text">IV-D</span> </span><span id="S4.SS4.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Sensitivity Analysis</span></h3>

<div id="S4.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We conduct a sensitivity analysis to understand how robots allocate their focus on neighboring agents, the object, and the goal position <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>. Specifically, at each time step we compute the derivative of the <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> norm of the action vector with respect to all input observations, including those of the ego robot, the goal, the object, and the neighboring robot. A small gradient magnitude means that perturbing an observation produces minimal changes in action, indicating a lower impact on the robot’s decision-making process; conversely, a large magnitude suggests a greater impact.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The saliency maps in <a href="#S4.F6" title="In IV-B Hardware Experiments ‣ IV Results ‣ Collaborative Loco-Manipulation for Pick-and-Place Tasks with Dynamic Reward Curriculum" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> show that the robots dynamically shift their attention to different elements of the environment depending on the task phase. During the approaching stage (A), the ego robot attends to both object handles and the neighboring robot’s base to decide on handle assignment. At the grasping and lifting stages (B), the ego robot focuses the most on its gripper, the handle it is grasping, and the neighboring robot’s gripper, ensuring coordinated lifting. As the robots transport the object to the goal (C), most of the attention shifts to the gripper and the goal position. When nearing the release stage (D), the ego robot pays little direct attention to its neighbor and instead focuses on the handle held by the partner (Handle 1). This suggests it learns to infer the neighbor’s motion through the handle, exploiting their physical coupling. Such reactivity enables the robots to respond effectively to changing circumstances, enhancing their coordination, efficiency, and overall task success.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S4.SS5.6" class="ltx_text">IV-E</span> </span><span id="S4.SS5.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Ablation Study on Dynamic Reward Curriculum</span></h3>

<div id="S4.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We conduct an ablation study of the dynamic reward curriculum on a 4-stage <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">EE</abbr> position tracking problem—see <a href="#S4.F7" title="In IV-F Benchmark Comparison ‣ IV Results ‣ Collaborative Loco-Manipulation for Pick-and-Place Tasks with Dynamic Reward Curriculum" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>. Although each stage involves the same type of task (tracking positions), the challenge lies in the long-horizon nature of the problem. As robots progress to later stages, their rewards become harder to tune because earlier stage rewards grow too large and dominate gradient optimization. The ablated method “Liu et al. (max)” follows <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al. [20]</a></cite>: once a stage is completed, its rewards are deactivated and set to the maximum values to encourage agents to complete more stages.
<a href="#S4.F7" title="In IV-F Benchmark Comparison ‣ IV Results ‣ Collaborative Loco-Manipulation for Pick-and-Place Tasks with Dynamic Reward Curriculum" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> shows that “Liu et al. (max)” gets stuck at <span id="S4.SS5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">S3</span> for about 3000 iterations, since large rewards from earlier stages make it hard for the optimizer to pick up the initially small rewards for <span id="S4.SS5.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">S4</span>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">As another variant, we set rewards from completed stages to the minimum value so the policy might more easily pick up new rewards; we call this ablated model “Liu et al. (min).” This version fails to pass even <span id="S4.SS5.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">S1</span>, as the policy prefers exploiting mastered rewards rather than transitioning to new stages with no immediate return. In summary, for long-horizon multi-stage <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">RL</abbr>, both ablated models suffer from difficult reward tuning, local minima, and poor sample efficiency. In contrast, our dynamic reward curriculum adaptively adjusts stage rewards during training, mitigating these issues and yielding higher task success and faster completion times, as shown in <a href="#S4.F7" title="In IV-F Benchmark Comparison ‣ IV Results ‣ Collaborative Loco-Manipulation for Pick-and-Place Tasks with Dynamic Reward Curriculum" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For completeness, we also train the ablated model (<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al. [20]</a></cite>) on the single-agent <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">PnP</abbr> problem. As shown in <a href="#S4.F8" title="In IV-F Benchmark Comparison ‣ IV Results ‣ Collaborative Loco-Manipulation for Pick-and-Place Tasks with Dynamic Reward Curriculum" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a> and <a href="#S4.T2" title="In IV-F Benchmark Comparison ‣ IV Results ‣ Collaborative Loco-Manipulation for Pick-and-Place Tasks with Dynamic Reward Curriculum" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">II</span></a>,it stalls after reaching <span id="S4.SS5.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">S4</span>, for the same reasons discussed above.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS6" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S4.SS6.6" class="ltx_text">IV-F</span> </span><span id="S4.SS6.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Benchmark Comparison</span></h3>

<figure id="S4.F7" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.13239v1/extra_ablation_exp.png" id="S4.F7.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/292;" width="476" height="292" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 7: </span>
Ablation of the dynamic reward curriculum on a 4-stage <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">EE</abbr> position-tracking task. The robot sequentially tracks points 1–4 with its gripper.
</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S4.F8" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.13239v1/experiment_data_comparison.png" id="S4.F8.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/289;" width="476" height="289" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 8: </span>
Mean end-of-episode stage number for single-agent <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">PnP</abbr> across different approaches. The <span id="S4.F8.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">y</span>-axis indicates the average stage number reached by the <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">HL</abbr> policy at the end of an episode, while the <span id="S4.F8.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">x</span>-axis shows the number of iterations required for the policy to reach that stage.
</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We benchmark our complete pipeline against two representative state-of-the-art methods that train separate policies for multi-stage <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">RL</abbr> problems—<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bendikas et al. [5]</a></cite> and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Erskine and Lehnert [7]</a></cite>—on the single-agent <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">PnP</abbr> task. Both approaches incorporate critic functions of adjacent stages to encourage cross-stage coherence when training separate policies. The key distinction lies in the direction of this integration: <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citeauthor"><a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bendikas et al.</a></cite> include critics from earlier stages and train each policy to execute all preceding sub-tasks, using only the final-stage policy at inference time. In contrast, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citeauthor"><a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Erskine and Lehnert</a></cite> optimize each policy solely for its assigned stage while incorporating the next-stage critic to guide training.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T2" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">TABLE II: </span>Performance metrics of different methods averaged over 500 trials in simulation.</figcaption>
